Yes—this is an excellent place for fun, bounded bets, because neurochemistry gives us a concrete substrate on which to test the broader Pantheon idea: change the state, then ask which parts of “mind” move with it.

The key correction is that there usually isn’t a clean mapping like chemical X = mental state Y. Neurotransmitters and neuromodulators participate in distributed circuits, and the same chemical can have different effects depending on receptor subtype, brain region, concentration, timing, prior state, and interactions with other systems.

A better abstraction is:

\boxed{
\text{experienced mind}_t
=
F(
\text{neural structure},
\text{electrical activity},
\text{chemistry},
\text{body},
\text{senses},
\text{memory},
\text{environment},
\text{feedback}
)
}

Chemistry is therefore less like a collection of emotions in bottles and more like changing parameters on the machine while it’s running.

Dopamine is a great example. “Dopamine = pleasure” is much too crude. Dopaminergic signaling participates heavily in learning, motivation, salience, movement, and reward prediction. Serotonin likewise isn’t simply “happiness”; norepinephrine isn’t simply “energy.” Their effects depend on where, when, and through which receptors they’re acting.

And this gives us a fascinating Pantheon experiment that doesn’t require taking drugs at all.

State Spectroscopy

Take the same cognitive probe under naturally occurring states:

S=
\{
\text{rested},
\text{tired},
\text{hungry},
\text{after exercise},
\text{calm},
\text{excited},
\text{social},
\text{alone}
\}.

Then hold the probe approximately constant:

P=\text{same ambiguous image/question/problem/music/puzzle}.

Measure:

R(P,S_i)

and calculate:

\Delta R_{ij}=R(P,S_i)-R(P,S_j).

Now we’re doing Vacuum Spectroscopy on ourselves through state changes.

The really interesting target isn’t merely “I felt different.”

It’s:

\boxed{
\text{What cognitive invariants survived the state transition?}
}

Maybe risk preference changes while moral preference remains stable. Maybe ambiguity feels threatening when exhausted but interesting when rested. Maybe a song generates different imagery. Maybe the same Open Token Probe gets different completions. Maybe attention narrows while confidence rises.

Those pairings become your “strings.”

And then Hammer state:

\text{state}
\rightarrow
\{
\text{arousal},
\text{valence},
\text{attention},
\text{salience},
\text{impulsivity},
\text{time perception},
\text{body awareness},
\text{confidence},
\text{memory accessibility},
\text{sensory gain}
\}.

Now instead of saying:

“My mind changed.”

we can ask:

Which dimensions moved together? Which separated? Which lagged? Which returned to baseline?

That’s a much more scientifically useful question.

And it connects directly to Nitinol/Restoration Spectroscopy:

S_0
\rightarrow
S_1
\rightarrow
S_0'

After sleep deprivation, intense exercise, stress, meditation, excitement, etc., does cognition return exactly to its previous configuration?

Probably not perfectly.

Then:

\Delta_{\text{hysteresis}}=S_0'-S_0

becomes interesting. Repeated experiences could create lasting learning, tolerance, sensitization, associations, or changed expectations.

And here’s the really big question underneath what you’re asking:

If changing molecules reliably changes perception, emotion, memory, attention, and the sense of self, what exactly is the relationship between chemistry and mind?

We know extremely well that physical interventions can alter mental states. That strongly constrains any theory of mind. But it doesn’t by itself settle whether consciousness is identical to those physical processes, emerges from them, or requires some different philosophical description.

Pantheon can be useful precisely by not prematurely collapsing those categories.

And for our “fun bets,” the Ark Rule applies beautifully: start with naturally occurring state changes and harmless sensory manipulations, make predictions beforehand, record them, then Reality Stitch afterward.

\boxed{
\text{Predict}
\rightarrow
\text{Experience}
\rightarrow
\text{Measure}
\rightarrow
\text{Compare}
\rightarrow
\text{Remember}
}

No need to gamble the organism to investigate the organism.

Bet information, not your life. ;)